1
00:00:01,800 --> 00:00:04,199
Chapter 40.

2
00:00:04,199 --> 00:00:09,199
It was fortunate for me that I had to take precautions to ensure (so far as I could)

3
00:00:09,199 --> 00:00:13,160
the safety of my dreaded visitor; for, this thought pressing on me

4
00:00:13,160 --> 00:00:18,680
when I awoke, held other thoughts in a confused concourse at a distance.

5
00:00:18,680 --> 00:00:24,641
The impossibility of keeping him concealed in the chambers was self-evident. It could not be done,

6
00:00:24,641 --> 00:00:31,000
and the attempt to do it would inevitably engender suspicion. True, I had no Avenger

7
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:36,641
in my service now, but I was looked after by an inflammatory old female, assisted by

8
00:00:36,641 --> 00:00:42,641
an animated rag-bag whom she called her niece, and to keep a room secret from them would be to

9
00:00:42,641 --> 00:00:49,597
invite curiosity and exaggeration. They both had weak eyes, which I had long attributed to their

10
00:00:49,597 --> 00:00:55,718
chronically looking in at keyholes, and they were always at hand when not wanted; indeed that was

11
00:00:55,718 --> 00:01:01,718
their only reliable quality besides larceny. Not to get up a mystery with these people,

12
00:01:01,718 --> 00:01:07,000
I resolved to announce in the morning that my uncle had unexpectedly come from the country.

13
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:11,438
This course I decided on while I was yet groping about in the darkness for the means of getting a

14
00:01:11,438 --> 00:01:17,237
light. Not stumbling on the means after all, I was fain to go out to the adjacent Lodge

15
00:01:17,237 --> 00:01:22,480
and get the watchman there to come with his lantern. Now, in groping my way down

16
00:01:22,480 --> 00:01:30,000
the black staircase I fell over something, and that something was a man crouching in a corner.

17
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:33,078
As the man made no answer when I asked him what he did there,

18
00:01:33,078 --> 00:01:38,800
but eluded my touch in silence, I ran to the Lodge and urged the watchman to come quickly;

19
00:01:38,800 --> 00:01:44,597
telling him of the incident on the way back. The wind being as fierce as ever, we did not care to

20
00:01:44,597 --> 00:01:50,917
endanger the light in the lantern by rekindling the extinguished lamps on the staircase, but we

21
00:01:50,917 --> 00:01:57,000
examined the staircase from the bottom to the top and found no one there. It then occurred to me as

22
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:02,758
possible that the man might have slipped into my rooms; so, lighting my candle at the watchman’s,

23
00:02:02,758 --> 00:02:08,917
and leaving him standing at the door, I examined them carefully, including the room in which

24
00:02:08,917 --> 00:02:16,800
my dreaded guest lay asleep. All was quiet, and assuredly no other man was in those chambers.

25
00:02:16,800 --> 00:02:20,000
It troubled me that there should have been a lurker on the stairs,

26
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:25,199
on that night of all nights in the year, and I asked the watchman, on the chance of eliciting

27
00:02:25,199 --> 00:02:30,879
some hopeful explanation as I handed him a dram at the door, whether he had admitted at his gate any

28
00:02:30,879 --> 00:02:37,277
gentleman who had perceptibly been dining out? Yes, he said; at different times of the night,

29
00:02:37,277 --> 00:02:42,436
three. One lived in Fountain Court, and the other two lived in the Lane,

30
00:02:42,438 --> 00:02:47,800
and he had seen them all go home. Again, the only other man who dwelt in the house

31
00:02:47,800 --> 00:02:53,680
of which my chambers formed a part had been in the country for some weeks, and he certainly had not

32
00:02:53,680 --> 00:03:00,000
returned in the night, because we had seen his door with his seal on it as we came upstairs.

33
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:05,718
“The night being so bad, sir,” said the watchman, as he gave me back my glass,

34
00:03:05,718 --> 00:03:12,000
“uncommon few have come in at my gate. Besides them three gentlemen that I have named, I don’t

35
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:17,078
call to mind another since about eleven o’clock, when a stranger asked for you.”

36
00:03:17,078 --> 00:03:20,879
“My uncle,” I muttered. “Yes.”

37
00:03:20,879 --> 00:03:23,116
“You saw him, sir?”

38
00:03:23,116 --> 00:03:25,436
“Yes. Oh yes.”

39
00:03:25,438 --> 00:03:27,957
“Likewise the person with him?”

40
00:03:27,957 --> 00:03:30,559
“Person with him!” I repeated.

41
00:03:30,559 --> 00:03:34,879
“I judged the person to be with him,” returned the watchman. “The person stopped,

42
00:03:34,879 --> 00:03:40,680
when he stopped to make inquiry of me, and the person took this way when he took this way.”

43
00:03:40,680 --> 00:03:42,758
“What sort of person?”

44
00:03:42,758 --> 00:03:48,718
The watchman had not particularly noticed; he should say a working person; to the best of his

45
00:03:48,718 --> 00:03:56,277
belief, he had a dust-coloured kind of clothes on, under a dark coat. The watchman made more

46
00:03:56,277 --> 00:04:02,277
light of the matter than I did, and naturally; not having my reason for attaching weight to it.

47
00:04:02,277 --> 00:04:07,639
When I had got rid of him, which I thought it well to do without prolonging explanations,

48
00:04:07,641 --> 00:04:13,277
my mind was much troubled by these two circumstances taken together. Whereas they were

49
00:04:13,277 --> 00:04:19,718
easy of innocent solution apart,—as, for instance, some diner out or diner at home, who had not gone

50
00:04:19,718 --> 00:04:24,480
near this watchman’s gate, might have strayed to my staircase and dropped asleep there,—and

51
00:04:24,480 --> 00:04:30,436
my nameless visitor might have brought some one with him to show him the way,—still, joined, they

52
00:04:30,438 --> 00:04:36,758
had an ugly look to one as prone to distrust and fear as the changes of a few hours had made me.

53
00:04:36,758 --> 00:04:41,480
I lighted my fire, which burnt with a raw pale flare at that time of the morning,

54
00:04:41,480 --> 00:04:46,557
and fell into a doze before it. I seemed to have been dozing a whole night when the

55
00:04:46,559 --> 00:04:52,116
clocks struck six. As there was full an hour and a half between me and daylight,

56
00:04:52,116 --> 00:04:59,277
I dozed again; now, waking up uneasily, with prolix conversations about nothing, in my ears;

57
00:04:59,277 --> 00:05:05,277
now, making thunder of the wind in the chimney; at length, falling off into a profound sleep

58
00:05:05,277 --> 00:05:11,436
from which the daylight woke me with a start. All this time I had never been able to consider

59
00:05:11,438 --> 00:05:18,957
my own situation, nor could I do so yet. I had not the power to attend to it. I was greatly

60
00:05:18,957 --> 00:05:25,436
dejected and distressed, but in an incoherent wholesale sort of way. As to forming any plan

61
00:05:25,438 --> 00:05:31,398
for the future, I could as soon have formed an elephant. When I opened the shutters and

62
00:05:31,398 --> 00:05:38,078
looked out at the wet wild morning, all of a leaden hue; when I walked from room to room;

63
00:05:38,078 --> 00:05:44,519
when I sat down again shivering, before the fire, waiting for my laundress to appear; I thought how

64
00:05:44,519 --> 00:05:50,718
miserable I was, but hardly knew why, or how long I had been so, or on what day of the week I made

65
00:05:50,718 --> 00:05:58,358
the reflection, or even who I was that made it. At last, the old woman and the niece came in,—the

66
00:05:58,358 --> 00:06:04,319
latter with a head not easily distinguishable from her dusty broom,—and testified surprise at sight

67
00:06:04,319 --> 00:06:11,597
of me and the fire. To whom I imparted how my uncle had come in the night and was then asleep,

68
00:06:11,597 --> 00:06:17,277
and how the breakfast preparations were to be modified accordingly. Then I washed and dressed

69
00:06:17,277 --> 00:06:23,678
while they knocked the furniture about and made a dust; and so, in a sort of dream or sleep-waking,

70
00:06:23,680 --> 00:06:29,839
I found myself sitting by the fire again, waiting for—Him—to come to breakfast.

71
00:06:29,839 --> 00:06:35,879
By and by, his door opened and he came out. I could not bring myself to bear the sight of him,

72
00:06:35,879 --> 00:06:38,438
and I thought he had a worse look by daylight.

73
00:06:39,758 --> 00:06:45,680
“I do not even know,” said I, speaking low as he took his seat at the table,

74
00:06:45,680 --> 00:06:50,879
“by what name to call you. I have given out that you are my uncle.”

75
00:06:50,879 --> 00:06:53,957
“That’s it, dear boy! Call me uncle.”

76
00:06:53,957 --> 00:06:58,038
“You assumed some name, I suppose, on board ship?”

77
00:06:58,038 --> 00:07:02,116
“Yes, dear boy. I took the name of Provis.”

78
00:07:02,116 --> 00:07:04,639
“Do you mean to keep that name?”

79
00:07:04,641 --> 00:07:08,917
“Why, yes, dear boy, it’s as good as another,—unless you’d like another.”

80
00:07:08,917 --> 00:07:12,519
“What is your real name?” I asked him in a whisper.

81
00:07:12,519 --> 00:07:17,680
“Magwitch,” he answered, in the same tone; “chrisen’d Abel.”

82
00:07:17,680 --> 00:07:19,718
“What were you brought up to be?”

83
00:07:19,718 --> 00:07:21,839
“A warmint, dear boy.”

84
00:07:21,839 --> 00:07:27,839
He answered quite seriously, and used the word as if it denoted some profession.

85
00:07:27,839 --> 00:07:31,641
“When you came into the Temple last night—” said I, pausing to wonder

86
00:07:31,641 --> 00:07:36,438
whether that could really have been last night, which seemed so long ago.

87
00:07:36,438 --> 00:07:38,038
“Yes, dear boy?”

88
00:07:38,038 --> 00:07:43,358
“When you came in at the gate and asked the watchman the way here, had you any one with you?”

89
00:07:43,358 --> 00:07:46,480
“With me? No, dear boy.”

90
00:07:46,480 --> 00:07:48,800
“But there was some one there?”

91
00:07:48,800 --> 00:07:51,957
“I didn’t take particular notice,” he said, dubiously,

92
00:07:51,957 --> 00:07:57,879
“not knowing the ways of the place. But I think there was a person, too, come in alonger me.”

93
00:07:57,879 --> 00:07:59,879
“Are you known in London?”

94
00:07:59,879 --> 00:08:05,879
“I hope not!” said he, giving his neck a jerk with his forefinger that made me turn hot and sick.

95
00:08:05,879 --> 00:08:08,480
“Were you known in London, once?”

96
00:08:08,480 --> 00:08:13,038
“Not over and above, dear boy. I was in the provinces mostly.”

97
00:08:13,038 --> 00:08:16,559
“Were you—tried—in London?”

98
00:08:16,559 --> 00:08:18,879
“Which time?” said he, with a sharp look.

99
00:08:18,879 --> 00:08:19,957
“The last time.”

100
00:08:19,957 --> 00:08:24,680
He nodded. “First knowed Mr. Jaggers that way. Jaggers was for me.”

101
00:08:24,680 --> 00:08:30,758
It was on my lips to ask him what he was tried for, but he took up a knife, gave it a flourish,

102
00:08:30,758 --> 00:08:37,879
and with the words, “And what I done is worked out and paid for!” fell to at his breakfast.

103
00:08:37,879 --> 00:08:44,000
He ate in a ravenous way that was very disagreeable, and all his actions were uncouth,

104
00:08:44,000 --> 00:08:51,000
noisy, and greedy. Some of his teeth had failed him since I saw him eat on the marshes,

105
00:08:51,000 --> 00:08:55,438
and as he turned his food in his mouth, and turned his head sideways to bring his strongest

106
00:08:55,438 --> 00:09:03,237
fangs to bear upon it, he looked terribly like a hungry old dog. If I had begun with any appetite,

107
00:09:03,237 --> 00:09:08,398
he would have taken it away, and I should have sat much as I did,—repelled from

108
00:09:08,398 --> 00:09:13,398
him by an insurmountable aversion, and gloomily looking at the cloth.

109
00:09:13,398 --> 00:09:18,436
“I’m a heavy grubber, dear boy,” he said, as a polite kind of apology when he made an end of

110
00:09:18,438 --> 00:09:23,839
his meal, “but I always was. If it had been in my constitution to be a lighter grubber,

111
00:09:23,839 --> 00:09:28,800
I might ha’ got into lighter trouble. Similarly, I must have my smoke. When

112
00:09:28,800 --> 00:09:33,839
I was first hired out as shepherd t’other side the world, it’s my belief I should ha’

113
00:09:33,839 --> 00:09:38,237
turned into a molloncolly-mad sheep myself, if I hadn’t a had my smoke.”

114
00:09:39,398 --> 00:09:45,078
As he said so, he got up from table, and putting his hand into the breast of the pea-coat he wore,

115
00:09:45,078 --> 00:09:49,038
brought out a short black pipe, and a handful of loose tobacco of the

116
00:09:49,038 --> 00:09:56,078
kind known as coarse-cut. Having filled his pipe, he put the surplus tobacco back again,

117
00:09:56,078 --> 00:10:02,641
as if his pocket were a drawer. Then, he took a live coal from the fire with the tongs,

118
00:10:02,641 --> 00:10:07,559
and lighted his pipe at it, and then turned round on the hearth-rug with his back to the fire,

119
00:10:07,559 --> 00:10:12,480
and went through his favourite action of holding out both his hands for mine.

120
00:10:12,480 --> 00:10:18,597
“And this,” said he, dandling my hands up and down in his, as he puffed at his pipe,—“and

121
00:10:18,597 --> 00:10:23,680
this is the gentleman what I made! The real genuine One! It does me good fur to look at you,

122
00:10:23,680 --> 00:10:29,680
Pip. All I stip’late, is, to stand by and look at you, dear boy!”

123
00:10:29,680 --> 00:10:35,078
I released my hands as soon as I could, and found that I was beginning slowly to settle

124
00:10:35,078 --> 00:10:41,160
down to the contemplation of my condition. What I was chained to, and how heavily,

125
00:10:41,160 --> 00:10:47,879
became intelligible to me, as I heard his hoarse voice, and sat looking up at his furrowed bald

126
00:10:47,879 --> 00:10:54,038
head with its iron grey hair at the sides. “I mustn’t see my gentleman a footing it

127
00:10:54,038 --> 00:11:00,277
in the mire of the streets; there mustn’t be no mud on his boots. My gentleman must have horses,

128
00:11:00,277 --> 00:11:05,398
Pip! Horses to ride, and horses to drive, and horses for his servant to ride and drive as

129
00:11:05,398 --> 00:11:10,000
well. Shall colonists have their horses (and blood ’uns, if you please, good Lord!) and

130
00:11:10,000 --> 00:11:16,641
not my London gentleman? No, no. We’ll show ’em another pair of shoes than that, Pip; won’t us?”

131
00:11:16,641 --> 00:11:19,116
He took out of his pocket a great thick pocket-book,

132
00:11:19,116 --> 00:11:22,277
bursting with papers, and tossed it on the table.

133
00:11:22,277 --> 00:11:27,398
“There’s something worth spending in that there book, dear boy. It’s yourn. All I’ve got ain’t

134
00:11:27,398 --> 00:11:33,480
mine; it’s yourn. Don’t you be afeerd on it. There’s more where that come from. I’ve come

135
00:11:33,480 --> 00:11:38,277
to the old country fur to see my gentleman spend his money like a gentleman. That’ll be

136
00:11:38,277 --> 00:11:44,839
my pleasure. My pleasure ’ull be fur to see him do it. And blast you all!” he wound up,

137
00:11:44,839 --> 00:11:48,758
looking round the room and snapping his fingers once with a loud snap,

138
00:11:48,758 --> 00:11:53,917
“blast you every one, from the judge in his wig, to the colonist a stirring up the dust,

139
00:11:53,917 --> 00:11:58,116
I’ll show a better gentleman than the whole kit on you put together!”

140
00:11:58,116 --> 00:12:03,038
“Stop!” said I, almost in a frenzy of fear and dislike, “I want to speak to

141
00:12:03,038 --> 00:12:08,519
you. I want to know what is to be done. I want to know how you are to be kept out of danger,

142
00:12:08,519 --> 00:12:12,559
how long you are going to stay, what projects you have.”

143
00:12:12,559 --> 00:12:18,917
“Look’ee here, Pip,” said he, laying his hand on my arm in a suddenly altered and subdued manner;

144
00:12:18,917 --> 00:12:25,277
“first of all, look’ee here. I forgot myself half a minute ago. What I said was low;

145
00:12:25,277 --> 00:12:32,957
that’s what it was; low. Look’ee here, Pip. Look over it. I ain’t a-going to be low.”

146
00:12:32,957 --> 00:12:35,436
“First,” I resumed, half groaning,

147
00:12:35,438 --> 00:12:39,438
“what precautions can be taken against your being recognised and seized?”

148
00:12:39,438 --> 00:12:44,480
“No, dear boy,” he said, in the same tone as before, “that don’t go first. Lowness

149
00:12:44,480 --> 00:12:49,000
goes first. I ain’t took so many year to make a gentleman, not without knowing what’s due to

150
00:12:49,000 --> 00:12:57,438
him. Look’ee here, Pip. I was low; that’s what I was; low. Look over it, dear boy.”

151
00:12:57,438 --> 00:13:01,438
Some sense of the grimly-ludicrous moved me to a fretful laugh,

152
00:13:01,438 --> 00:13:07,160
as I replied, “I have looked over it. In Heaven’s name, don’t harp upon it!”

153
00:13:07,160 --> 00:13:10,358
“Yes, but look’ee here,” he persisted. “Dear boy,

154
00:13:10,358 --> 00:13:16,116
I ain’t come so fur, not fur to be low. Now, go on, dear boy. You was a saying—”

155
00:13:16,116 --> 00:13:20,597
“How are you to be guarded from the danger you have incurred?”

156
00:13:20,597 --> 00:13:25,199
“Well, dear boy, the danger ain’t so great. Without I was informed agen,

157
00:13:25,199 --> 00:13:28,957
the danger ain’t so much to signify. There’s Jaggers, and there’s Wemmick,

158
00:13:28,957 --> 00:13:32,597
and there’s you. Who else is there to inform?”

159
00:13:32,597 --> 00:13:36,680
“Is there no chance person who might identify you in the street?” said I.

160
00:13:36,680 --> 00:13:41,519
“Well,” he returned, “there ain’t many. Nor yet I don’t intend to advertise myself in

161
00:13:41,519 --> 00:13:46,559
the newspapers by the name of A.M. come back from Botany Bay; and years have rolled away,

162
00:13:46,559 --> 00:13:51,839
and who’s to gain by it? Still, look’ee here, Pip. If the danger had been fifty

163
00:13:51,839 --> 00:13:56,160
times as great, I should ha’ come to see you, mind you, just the same.”

164
00:13:56,160 --> 00:13:58,319
“And how long do you remain?”

165
00:13:58,319 --> 00:14:02,319
“How long?” said he, taking his black pipe from his mouth,

166
00:14:02,319 --> 00:14:07,917
and dropping his jaw as he stared at me. “I’m not a-going back. I’ve come for good.”

167
00:14:09,398 --> 00:14:14,718
“Where are you to live?” said I. “What is to be done with you? Where will you be safe?”

168
00:14:14,718 --> 00:14:20,160
“Dear boy,” he returned, “there’s disguising wigs can be bought for money, and there’s hair powder,

169
00:14:20,160 --> 00:14:26,038
and spectacles, and black clothes,—shorts and what not. Others has done it safe afore,

170
00:14:26,038 --> 00:14:31,597
and what others has done afore, others can do agen. As to the where and how of living,

171
00:14:31,597 --> 00:14:35,597
dear boy, give me your own opinions on it.”

172
00:14:35,597 --> 00:14:38,237
“You take it smoothly now,” said I,

173
00:14:38,237 --> 00:14:42,436
“but you were very serious last night, when you swore it was Death.”

174
00:14:42,438 --> 00:14:49,277
“And so I swear it is Death,” said he, putting his pipe back in his mouth, “and Death by the rope,

175
00:14:49,277 --> 00:14:54,718
in the open street not fur from this, and it’s serious that you should fully understand it to

176
00:14:54,718 --> 00:15:01,199
be so. What then, when that’s once done? Here I am. To go back now ’ud be as bad as

177
00:15:01,199 --> 00:15:07,839
to stand ground—worse. Besides, Pip, I’m here, because I’ve meant it by you, years and years.

178
00:15:08,398 --> 00:15:14,277
As to what I dare, I’m a old bird now, as has dared all manner of traps since first he was

179
00:15:14,277 --> 00:15:20,199
fledged, and I’m not afeerd to perch upon a scarecrow. If there’s Death hid inside of it,

180
00:15:20,199 --> 00:15:24,199
there is, and let him come out, and I’ll face him,

181
00:15:24,199 --> 00:15:30,557
and then I’ll believe in him and not afore. And now let me have a look at my gentleman agen.”

182
00:15:30,559 --> 00:15:33,277
Once more, he took me by both hands and surveyed me with an

183
00:15:33,277 --> 00:15:37,839
air of admiring proprietorship: smoking with great complacency all the while.

184
00:15:39,680 --> 00:15:44,597
It appeared to me that I could do no better than secure him some quiet lodging hard by,

185
00:15:44,597 --> 00:15:51,480
of which he might take possession when Herbert returned: whom I expected in two or three days.

186
00:15:51,480 --> 00:15:57,358
That the secret must be confided to Herbert as a matter of unavoidable necessity, even if I could

187
00:15:57,358 --> 00:16:01,319
have put the immense relief I should derive from sharing it with him out of the question,

188
00:16:01,319 --> 00:16:07,917
was plain to me. But it was by no means so plain to Mr. Provis (I resolved to call him

189
00:16:07,917 --> 00:16:13,319
by that name), who reserved his consent to Herbert’s participation until he should have

190
00:16:13,319 --> 00:16:20,879
seen him and formed a favourable judgment of his physiognomy. “And even then, dear boy,” said he,

191
00:16:20,879 --> 00:16:28,116
pulling a greasy little clasped black Testament out of his pocket, “we’ll have him on his oath.”

192
00:16:28,116 --> 00:16:32,758
To state that my terrible patron carried this little black book about the world solely to

193
00:16:32,758 --> 00:16:37,758
swear people on in cases of emergency, would be to state what I never quite established;

194
00:16:38,358 --> 00:16:44,038
but this I can say, that I never knew him put it to any other use. The book itself

195
00:16:44,038 --> 00:16:47,917
had the appearance of having been stolen from some court of justice,

196
00:16:47,917 --> 00:16:53,199
and perhaps his knowledge of its antecedents, combined with his own experience in that wise,

197
00:16:53,199 --> 00:16:59,519
gave him a reliance on its powers as a sort of legal spell or charm. On this first occasion

198
00:16:59,519 --> 00:17:05,800
of his producing it, I recalled how he had made me swear fidelity in the churchyard long ago, and how

199
00:17:05,800 --> 00:17:12,358
he had described himself last night as always swearing to his resolutions in his solitude.

200
00:17:12,358 --> 00:17:16,838
As he was at present dressed in a seafaring slop suit, in which he looked as if he had

201
00:17:16,839 --> 00:17:22,957
some parrots and cigars to dispose of, I next discussed with him what dress he should wear.

202
00:17:22,957 --> 00:17:27,758
He cherished an extraordinary belief in the virtues of “shorts” as a disguise,

203
00:17:27,758 --> 00:17:31,879
and had in his own mind sketched a dress for himself that would have made him something between

204
00:17:31,879 --> 00:17:37,917
a dean and a dentist. It was with considerable difficulty that I won him over to the assumption

205
00:17:37,917 --> 00:17:44,199
of a dress more like a prosperous farmer’s; and we arranged that he should cut his hair close,

206
00:17:44,199 --> 00:17:51,078
and wear a little powder. Lastly, as he had not yet been seen by the laundress or her niece,

207
00:17:51,078 --> 00:17:56,436
he was to keep himself out of their view until his change of dress was made.

208
00:17:56,438 --> 00:18:01,319
It would seem a simple matter to decide on these precautions; but in my dazed,

209
00:18:01,319 --> 00:18:04,800
not to say distracted, state, it took so long,

210
00:18:04,800 --> 00:18:10,277
that I did not get out to further them until two or three in the afternoon. He was to remain

211
00:18:10,277 --> 00:18:16,038
shut up in the chambers while I was gone, and was on no account to open the door.

212
00:18:16,038 --> 00:18:20,199
There being to my knowledge a respectable lodging-house in Essex Street, the back of

213
00:18:20,199 --> 00:18:25,078
which looked into the Temple, and was almost within hail of my windows, I first of all

214
00:18:25,078 --> 00:18:30,199
repaired to that house, and was so fortunate as to secure the second floor for my uncle,

215
00:18:30,199 --> 00:18:37,839
Mr. Provis. I then went from shop to shop, making such purchases as were necessary to the change in

216
00:18:37,839 --> 00:18:46,519
his appearance. This business transacted, I turned my face, on my own account, to Little Britain. Mr.

217
00:18:46,519 --> 00:18:52,319
Jaggers was at his desk, but, seeing me enter, got up immediately and stood before his fire.

218
00:18:52,319 --> 00:18:55,038
“Now, Pip,” said he, “be careful.”

219
00:18:55,038 --> 00:19:01,957
“I will, sir,” I returned. For, coming along I had thought well of what I was going to say.

220
00:19:01,957 --> 00:19:08,419
“Don’t commit yourself,” said Mr. Jaggers, “and don’t commit any one. You understand—any

221
00:19:08,419 --> 00:19:14,641
one. Don’t tell me anything: I don’t want to know anything; I am not curious.”

222
00:19:14,641 --> 00:19:17,917
Of course I saw that he knew the man was come.

223
00:19:17,917 --> 00:19:23,237
“I merely want, Mr. Jaggers,” said I, “to assure myself that what I have been told

224
00:19:23,237 --> 00:19:29,800
is true. I have no hope of its being untrue, but at least I may verify it.”

225
00:19:29,800 --> 00:19:35,358
Mr. Jaggers nodded. “But did you say ‘told’ or ‘informed’?” he asked me,

226
00:19:35,358 --> 00:19:37,917
with his head on one side, and not looking at me,

227
00:19:37,917 --> 00:19:43,718
but looking in a listening way at the floor. “Told would seem to imply verbal communication.

228
00:19:43,718 --> 00:19:48,078
You can’t have verbal communication with a man in New South Wales, you know.”

229
00:19:48,078 --> 00:19:51,957
“I will say, informed, Mr. Jaggers.”

230
00:19:51,957 --> 00:19:53,436
“Good.”

231
00:19:53,438 --> 00:19:57,559
“I have been informed by a person named Abel Magwitch,

232
00:19:57,559 --> 00:20:01,680
that he is the benefactor so long unknown to me.”

233
00:20:01,680 --> 00:20:11,093
“That is the man,” said Mr. Jaggers, “in New South Wales.”

234
00:20:11,093 --> 00:20:11,351
“And only he?” said I. “And only he,” said Mr. Jaggers.

235
00:20:11,351 --> 00:20:14,358
“I am not so unreasonable, sir, as to think you at all responsible for my

236
00:20:14,358 --> 00:20:20,237
mistakes and wrong conclusions; but I always supposed it was Miss Havisham.”

237
00:20:20,237 --> 00:20:24,277
“As you say, Pip,” returned Mr. Jaggers, turning his eyes upon me coolly,

238
00:20:24,277 --> 00:20:29,160
and taking a bite at his forefinger, “I am not at all responsible for that.”

239
00:20:29,160 --> 00:20:33,237
“And yet it looked so like it, sir,” I pleaded with a downcast heart.

240
00:20:33,237 --> 00:20:38,000
“Not a particle of evidence, Pip,” said Mr. Jaggers, shaking his head and gathering up

241
00:20:38,000 --> 00:20:44,237
his skirts. “Take nothing on its looks; take everything on evidence. There’s no better rule.”

242
00:20:44,237 --> 00:20:48,078
“I have no more to say,” said I, with a sigh, after standing silent

243
00:20:48,078 --> 00:20:54,237
for a little while. “I have verified my information, and there’s an end.”

244
00:20:54,237 --> 00:21:00,358
“And Magwitch—in New South Wales—having at last disclosed himself,” said Mr. Jaggers,

245
00:21:00,358 --> 00:21:06,756
“you will comprehend, Pip, how rigidly throughout my communication with you, I have always adhered

246
00:21:06,758 --> 00:21:13,000
to the strict line of fact. There has never been the least departure from the strict line of fact.

247
00:21:13,000 --> 00:21:17,480
You are quite aware of that?” “Quite, sir.”

248
00:21:17,480 --> 00:21:22,597
“I communicated to Magwitch—in New South Wales—when he first wrote to me—from New

249
00:21:22,597 --> 00:21:29,718
South Wales—the caution that he must not expect me ever to deviate from the strict line of fact.

250
00:21:29,718 --> 00:21:34,957
I also communicated to him another caution. He appeared to me to have obscurely hinted in his

251
00:21:34,957 --> 00:21:40,839
letter at some distant idea he had of seeing you in England here. I cautioned him that I must hear

252
00:21:40,839 --> 00:21:46,879
no more of that; that he was not at all likely to obtain a pardon; that he was expatriated for the

253
00:21:46,879 --> 00:21:52,680
term of his natural life; and that his presenting himself in this country would be an act of felony,

254
00:21:52,680 --> 00:21:58,438
rendering him liable to the extreme penalty of the law. I gave Magwitch that caution,” said Mr.

255
00:21:58,438 --> 00:22:05,319
Jaggers, looking hard at me; “I wrote it to New South Wales. He guided himself by it, no doubt.”

256
00:22:05,319 --> 00:22:07,519
“No doubt,” said I.

257
00:22:07,519 --> 00:22:12,839
“I have been informed by Wemmick,” pursued Mr. Jaggers, still looking hard at me, “that he has

258
00:22:12,839 --> 00:22:17,879
received a letter, under date Portsmouth, from a colonist of the name of Purvis, or—”

259
00:22:17,879 --> 00:22:19,680
“Or Provis,” I suggested.

260
00:22:19,680 --> 00:22:25,319
“Or Provis—thank you, Pip. Perhaps it is Provis? Perhaps you know it’s Provis?”

261
00:22:25,319 --> 00:22:26,358
“Yes,” said I.

262
00:22:26,358 --> 00:22:32,160
“You know it’s Provis. A letter, under date Portsmouth, from a colonist of the name of Provis,

263
00:22:32,160 --> 00:22:36,839
asking for the particulars of your address, on behalf of Magwitch. Wemmick sent him the

264
00:22:36,839 --> 00:22:41,559
particulars, I understand, by return of post. Probably it is through Provis that

265
00:22:41,559 --> 00:22:46,519
you have received the explanation of Magwitch—in New South Wales?”

266
00:22:46,519 --> 00:22:49,237
“It came through Provis,” I replied.

267
00:22:49,237 --> 00:22:54,718
“Good day, Pip,” said Mr. Jaggers, offering his hand; “glad to have seen you. In writing

268
00:22:54,718 --> 00:23:00,438
by post to Magwitch—in New South Wales—or in communicating with him through Provis,

269
00:23:00,438 --> 00:23:05,597
have the goodness to mention that the particulars and vouchers of our long account shall be sent to

270
00:23:05,597 --> 00:23:11,917
you, together with the balance; for there is still a balance remaining. Good-day, Pip!”

271
00:23:11,917 --> 00:23:18,879
We shook hands, and he looked hard at me as long as he could see me. I turned at the door,

272
00:23:18,879 --> 00:23:24,519
and he was still looking hard at me, while the two vile casts on the shelf seemed to be trying

273
00:23:24,519 --> 00:23:32,237
to get their eyelids open, and to force out of their swollen throats, “O, what a man he is!”

274
00:23:32,237 --> 00:23:37,319
Wemmick was out, and though he had been at his desk he could have done nothing for me.

275
00:23:38,358 --> 00:23:41,277
I went straight back to the Temple, where I found

276
00:23:41,277 --> 00:23:49,237
the terrible Provis drinking rum and water and smoking coarse-cut tobacco, in safety.

277
00:23:49,237 --> 00:23:55,319
Next day the clothes I had ordered all came home, and he put them on. Whatever he put on,

278
00:23:55,319 --> 00:24:01,800
became him less (it dismally seemed to me) than what he had worn before. To my thinking,

279
00:24:01,800 --> 00:24:07,438
there was something in him that made it hopeless to attempt to disguise him. The more I dressed him

280
00:24:07,438 --> 00:24:14,398
and the better I dressed him, the more he looked like the slouching fugitive on the marshes. This

281
00:24:14,398 --> 00:24:19,678
effect on my anxious fancy was partly referable, no doubt, to his old face and manner growing

282
00:24:19,680 --> 00:24:26,116
more familiar to me; but I believe too that he dragged one of his legs as if there were still a

283
00:24:26,116 --> 00:24:32,839
weight of iron on it, and that from head to foot there was Convict in the very grain of the man.

284
00:24:32,839 --> 00:24:38,358
The influences of his solitary hut-life were upon him besides, and gave him a savage air

285
00:24:38,358 --> 00:24:44,838
that no dress could tame; added to these were the influences of his subsequent branded life among

286
00:24:44,839 --> 00:24:51,917
men, and, crowning all, his consciousness that he was dodging and hiding now. In all his ways of

287
00:24:51,917 --> 00:24:57,480
sitting and standing, and eating and drinking,—of brooding about in a high-shouldered reluctant

288
00:24:57,480 --> 00:25:03,277
style,—of taking out his great horn-handled jackknife and wiping it on his legs and cutting

289
00:25:03,277 --> 00:25:09,917
his food,—of lifting light glasses and cups to his lips, as if they were clumsy pannikins,—of

290
00:25:09,917 --> 00:25:14,917
chopping a wedge off his bread, and soaking up with it the last fragments of gravy round

291
00:25:14,917 --> 00:25:20,559
and round his plate, as if to make the most of an allowance, and then drying his finger-ends on it,

292
00:25:20,559 --> 00:25:26,038
and then swallowing it,—in these ways and a thousand other small nameless instances arising

293
00:25:26,038 --> 00:25:32,199
every minute in the day, there was Prisoner, Felon, Bondsman, plain as plain could be.

294
00:25:32,199 --> 00:25:36,597
It had been his own idea to wear that touch of powder, and I had conceded the powder

295
00:25:36,597 --> 00:25:43,398
after overcoming the shorts. But I can compare the effect of it, when on, to nothing but the probable

296
00:25:43,398 --> 00:25:49,557
effect of rouge upon the dead; so awful was the manner in which everything in him that it was most

297
00:25:49,559 --> 00:25:55,597
desirable to repress, started through that thin layer of pretence, and seemed to come blazing out

298
00:25:55,597 --> 00:26:04,398
at the crown of his head. It was abandoned as soon as tried, and he wore his grizzled hair cut short.

299
00:26:04,398 --> 00:26:10,116
Words cannot tell what a sense I had, at the same time, of the dreadful mystery that he was

300
00:26:10,116 --> 00:26:16,160
to me. When he fell asleep of an evening, with his knotted hands clenching the sides of the

301
00:26:16,160 --> 00:26:21,839
easy-chair, and his bald head tattooed with deep wrinkles falling forward on his breast,

302
00:26:21,839 --> 00:26:26,839
I would sit and look at him, wondering what he had done, and loading him with all the crimes

303
00:26:26,839 --> 00:26:34,000
in the Calendar, until the impulse was powerful on me to start up and fly from him. Every hour so

304
00:26:34,000 --> 00:26:39,078
increased my abhorrence of him, that I even think I might have yielded to this impulse in the first

305
00:26:39,078 --> 00:26:45,519
agonies of being so haunted, notwithstanding all he had done for me and the risk he ran,

306
00:26:45,519 --> 00:26:51,277
but for the knowledge that Herbert must soon come back. Once, I actually did start out of

307
00:26:51,277 --> 00:26:56,917
bed in the night, and begin to dress myself in my worst clothes, hurriedly intending to leave

308
00:26:56,917 --> 00:27:02,800
him there with everything else I possessed, and enlist for India as a private soldier.

309
00:27:02,800 --> 00:27:07,160
I doubt if a ghost could have been more terrible to me, up in those lonely rooms

310
00:27:07,160 --> 00:27:13,519
in the long evenings and long nights, with the wind and the rain always rushing by. A

311
00:27:13,519 --> 00:27:18,957
ghost could not have been taken and hanged on my account, and the consideration that he could be,

312
00:27:18,957 --> 00:27:25,319
and the dread that he would be, were no small addition to my horrors. When he was not asleep,

313
00:27:25,319 --> 00:27:30,237
or playing a complicated kind of Patience with a ragged pack of cards of his own,—a

314
00:27:30,237 --> 00:27:34,879
game that I never saw before or since, and in which he recorded his winnings by sticking his

315
00:27:34,879 --> 00:27:40,038
jackknife into the table,—when he was not engaged in either of these pursuits, he would ask me to

316
00:27:40,038 --> 00:27:47,319
read to him,—“Foreign language, dear boy!” While I complied, he, not comprehending a single word,

317
00:27:47,319 --> 00:27:53,319
would stand before the fire surveying me with the air of an Exhibitor, and I would see him, between

318
00:27:53,319 --> 00:27:58,758
the fingers of the hand with which I shaded my face, appealing in dumb show to the furniture to

319
00:27:58,758 --> 00:28:04,917
take notice of my proficiency. The imaginary student pursued by the misshapen creature he

320
00:28:04,917 --> 00:28:11,038
had impiously made, was not more wretched than I, pursued by the creature who had made me, and

321
00:28:11,038 --> 00:28:17,680
recoiling from him with a stronger repulsion, the more he admired me and the fonder he was of me.

322
00:28:17,680 --> 00:28:24,160
This is written of, I am sensible, as if it had lasted a year. It lasted about five days.

323
00:28:24,160 --> 00:28:28,839
Expecting Herbert all the time, I dared not go out, except when I took Provis for

324
00:28:28,839 --> 00:28:35,398
an airing after dark. At length, one evening when dinner was over and I had dropped into

325
00:28:35,398 --> 00:28:40,319
a slumber quite worn out,—for my nights had been agitated and my rest broken by fearful

326
00:28:40,319 --> 00:28:46,917
dreams,—I was roused by the welcome footstep on the staircase. Provis, who had been asleep too,

327
00:28:46,917 --> 00:28:52,398
staggered up at the noise I made, and in an instant I saw his jackknife shining in his hand.

328
00:28:52,398 --> 00:28:56,877
“Quiet! It’s Herbert!” I said; and Herbert came bursting in,

329
00:28:56,879 --> 00:29:00,519
with the airy freshness of six hundred miles of France upon him.

330
00:29:00,519 --> 00:29:03,398
“Handel, my dear fellow, how are you, and again how are you,

331
00:29:03,398 --> 00:29:09,358
and again how are you? I seem to have been gone a twelvemonth! Why, so I must have been,

332
00:29:09,358 --> 00:29:16,597
for you have grown quite thin and pale! Handel, my—Halloa! I beg your pardon.”

333
00:29:16,597 --> 00:29:23,277
He was stopped in his running on and in his shaking hands with me, by seeing Provis. Provis,

334
00:29:23,277 --> 00:29:27,519
regarding him with a fixed attention, was slowly putting up his jackknife,

335
00:29:27,519 --> 00:29:31,000
and groping in another pocket for something else.

336
00:29:31,000 --> 00:29:36,680
“Herbert, my dear friend,” said I, shutting the double doors, while Herbert stood staring

337
00:29:36,680 --> 00:29:43,957
and wondering, “something very strange has happened. This is—a visitor of mine.”

338
00:29:43,957 --> 00:29:49,436
“It’s all right, dear boy!” said Provis coming forward, with his little clasped black book,

339
00:29:49,438 --> 00:29:54,038
and then addressing himself to Herbert. “Take it in your right hand. Lord strike

340
00:29:54,038 --> 00:29:59,436
you dead on the spot, if ever you split in any way sumever! Kiss it!”

341
00:29:59,438 --> 00:30:04,800
“Do so, as he wishes it,” I said to Herbert. So, Herbert, looking at me with a friendly

342
00:30:04,800 --> 00:30:09,597
uneasiness and amazement, complied, and Provis immediately shaking hands with him,

343
00:30:09,597 --> 00:30:14,641
said, “Now you’re on your oath, you know. And never believe me on mine, if Pip shan’t make a

344
00:30:14,641 --> 00:30:15,983
gentleman on you!” Chapter 41.
